Search for later s matches with the text before them in view.
With the g flag or a number, s looked for each match after the first by
running the regex on the rest of the pattern space as a new string with
REG_NOTBOL. That keeps ^ from matching again, but the regex no longer
sees the character before the new start, so \<, \b, \B and \` judged it
as the start of the text: "s/\<a/X/g" turned "aaa a" into "XXX X"
instead of "Xaa X", "s/\b/X/g" put an X between every pair of letters,
and "s/\`a/X/g" replaced every a. Where REG_STARTEND is available, as
awk already does for gsub, search the whole pattern space from the
offset reached instead, still with REG_NOTBOL, and make the match
offsets relative to that offset again.

---
